- op: create  # aad7
  at: '2026-05-20T00:05:29Z'  # aad7
  by: agent  # aad7
  actor: jgstern_agent  # aad7
  clock: 1  # aad7
  nonce: aad7  # aad7
  data:  # aad7
    kind: invariant  # aad7
    title: "META: Behavior maps must carry PROV-compliant provenance enabling derivation queries and multi-source attribution"  # aad7
    status: violated  # aad7
    priority: 2  # aad7
    parent:  # aad7
    tags:  # aad7
    - analysis_quality  # aad7
    - schema  # aad7
    - spec_compliance  # aad7
    - concept_audit  # aad7
    isbefore: []  # aad7
    duplicate_of: []  # aad7
    not_duplicate_of: []  # aad7
    pr_ref:  # aad7
    description: "## Statement\n\nEvery Symbol and Edge in the behavior map should be queryable along three PROV-compliant dimensions:\n\n1. **Derivation** (`wasDerivedFrom`): given an Edge, which Symbols were consumed by the linker that produced it? This makes \"why does this edge exist?\" answerable without reading linker source code.\n2. **Attribution** (`wasAttributedTo`): when an Edge or Symbol results from evidence combined by multiple passes (analyzer locates the call-site, linker resolves the target), all contributing passes should be credited. Currently `Symbol.origin` and `Edge.origin` are scalar — only one pass gets the credit.\n3. **Process model** (`wasInformedBy`): pass-to-pass dependencies should be declared (`Pass.requires`) rather than implicit in run-ordering convention. This enables config-time validation of disabled-prerequisite errors and explicit parallelism.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe current provenance model captures `(execution_id, pass_id)` per Symbol/Edge but treats every other relationship implicitly:\n\n- Linker code reads `ctx.symbols` and decides how to construct an edge; the choice is opaque to consumers of the behavior map. There is no way to ask \"what symbols did the linker consume to produce this edge\" without re-running the linker logic.\n- `Symbol.origin: str` and `Edge.origin: str` are scalars — a single pass can be credited. When evidence is combined across passes, the model forces a single attribution choice, which is structurally lossy.\n- Pass ordering is implicit: linkers run after analyzers run after the orchestrator's file-symbol synthesizer. Each member of this chain knows its position by convention. There is no declarative dependency graph that the runtime checks.\n\nThese three gaps form a coherent architectural axis: behavior maps capture activity (passes ran) and effect (edges and symbols exist) but do not capture causation (how passes produced their results). PROV's standard vocabulary distinguishes these and we currently squash them into one scalar.\n\n## Members\n\n- **INV-prov-C** (or similar) — Add `Edge.derived_from: list[str]` (Symbol IDs the linker consumed to construct this edge). Touches every linker's edge-construction site. Enables `hypergumbo explain --edge <id>` to show the derivation chain.\n- **INV-prov-D** — Change `Symbol.origin` and `Edge.origin` from `string` to `list[string]`. Multi-source attribution; schema-breaking change (major bump). Touches IR + schema + every producer.\n- **INV-prov-E** — Add `Pass.requires: list[str]` declaring upstream pass IDs. Pre-run validator asserts every declared dependency exists in the active pass set. Enables parallel scheduling and config-time error detection.\n\n## Closure criterion\n\nThe META closes `satisfied` when:\n\n1. `Edge.derived_from` populates correctly on at least the linker subcategories with multi-symbol inputs (Protocol, Bridge, Framework, Infrastructure per ADR-0003-ext).\n2. `Symbol.origin` and `Edge.origin` are documented as lists in the schema; every producer in the codebase emits a list (single-element lists are valid and the migration story is documented).\n3. `Pass.requires` declarations exist on every linker pass; the pre-run validator runs in CI on hypergumbo's own pass set.\n4. `hypergumbo explain` surfaces derivation chains in its output.\n5. ADR documenting the PROV-vocabulary mapping (Entity / Activity / Agent / wasDerivedFrom / wasAttributedTo / wasInformedBy) is checked in.\n\n## Related METAs\n\n- `INV-rugok` (spec/schema/impl drift): about whether contracts match implementation. Orthogonal axis.\n- `INV-dajaf` (identity honesty): about whether IDs honestly identify what they claim to. Closer to this META's domain but distinct — identity is \"who am I\"; provenance is \"where do I come from\".\n\nThis META is the explainability/traceability axis. INV-rugok is the contract-conformance axis. INV-dajaf is the identity-axis. Together they cover most of what a \"behavior map\" needs to be honest about."  # aad7
    fields:  # aad7
      statement: "Every Symbol and Edge should be queryable along three PROV dimensions: derivation (wasDerivedFrom — which Symbols did the linker consume?), attribution (wasAttributedTo — which passes contributed evidence?), and process model (wasInformedBy — which other passes must run first?). Today origin is scalar, derivation chains are implicit in linker source code, and pass dependencies are convention-based not declared."  # aad7
      root_cause: "The current provenance model captures (execution_id, pass_id) per Symbol/Edge but squashes derivation, attribution, and dependency into a single scalar 'origin' field. Linker code reads ctx.symbols and decides how to construct an edge; the choice is opaque to consumers. Multi-pass evidence combination forces a single-attribution choice. Pass ordering is implicit."  # aad7
